l_set*`, no `test_navigator*content_id*`, nothing in `tests_sam/test_progressive_markdown.py` or `backlog_core/tests/unit/progressive_markdown/` referencing content identity, hashing, or SQLite-backed caching. This is 100% new test surface, on both the storage module and the (previously omitted) read-path parameter/response-shape change.\\n- **Test path registration**: whichever directory the new module's tests land in must already be covered by `pyproject.toml`'s `[tool.pytest.ini_options] testpaths` (currently: `plugins/development-harness/tests`, `tests_sam`, `sam_schema/tests`, `backlog_core/tests` \u2014 no `progressive_markdown/tests` entry exists) and by `run_pytest.py`'s `_DEFAULT_TEST_PATHS` list (`tests`, `tests_sam`, `sam_schema/tests`, `backlog_core/tests`). **If new tests are placed under a `progressive_markdown/tests/` directory (mirroring `backlog_core/tests/` and `sam_schema/tests/` precedent) rather than the shared `tests/` or `tests_sam/`, both files need a new entry** \u2014 this is a required task item, not incidental.\\n- **`pyproject.toml`'s `[tool.ty] extra-paths`** \u2014 same category of miss risk: if the new module needs a directory `ty` doesn't already resolve (unlikely if it lives directly in `progressive_markdown/`, which likely already resolves via existing entries, but must be verified \u2014 not assumed) it needs an `extra-paths` entry, per the documented `unresolved-import`/`unresolved-attribute` failure pattern in this repo's Python-development conventions.\\n- **Eviction/cleanup timing tests** need a controllable clock or injectable `last_cleanup_at` rather than real hourly-to-daily sleeps \u2014 a design constraint for testability the architect spec should address explicitly, not left implicit.\\n\\n## 6. Configuration and CI\\n\\n- **No `.github/workflows/*.yml` currently references** `progressive_markdown`, `control-set`, or `control_set` (confirmed via grep \u2014 zero matches). No CI job needs a new entry purely for source-file presence; `lint-python`/`typecheck-ty`/`test-python` already run repo-wide.\\n- **No new environment variable is implied by ADR-3082-1** beyond what `state_root()`/`DH_STATE_HOME` already resolves (confirmed: ADR explicitly rejects a `$DH_STATE_HOME`-global path in favor of per-project `state_root()`, so no new override variable is being introduced).\\n- **`.dh/config.yaml` / `backlog.backend` selection is unaffected** \u2014 the control set is Navigation-layer cache state, explicitly NOT part of `WorkItemBackend`/`TaskBackend` selection (ADR-3082-1 Consequences: \\\"distinct from any per-plugin provider database... must not be added to that schema\\\"). No backend-provider config surface changes.\\n- **40MB soft target and hourly-to-daily cleanup cadence are both explicitly flagged in ADR-3082-1 as unsourced starting defaults** (\\\"should be revisited under real usage\\\") \u2014 if either becomes a tunable, it needs a config surface decision during planning; not assumed to require one now.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Scope-expanding findings for the orchestrator** (no active grooming-swarm team session exists for this item; reporting directly per dispatch instructions):\\n\\n1. **`backlog_view` does not currently route through `progressive_markdown`'s engine at all** (section 1b). Confirmed by grep: zero references to `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` in `backlog_core/server.py`; `backlog_view` uses its own local pagination logic instead \u2014 exactly the duplicate `component-architecture.md` says is \\\"scheduled for deletion, not maintenance,\\\" with no tracked migration issue found. Wiring `content_id` into the engine alone does not automatically make it visible to `backlog_view`'s live traffic. Planning must decide explicitly whether that migration is in scope for #3062, a named prerequisite, or a named follow-up \u2014 silently assuming either answer risks shipping a control set nothing production-facing reads from.\\n2. **`task_status_hook.py`'s actual invalidation mechanism is an open design question, not a confirmed requirement.** The discovery artifact treats \\\"task_status_hook.py will need to call into this same invalidation entrypoint\\\" as settled (per a cross-session architectural-alignment check). This analysis found the hook writes exclusively through a `fastmcp call` subprocess to `sam_task`, never importing `dh_core`/`sam_schema` Python internals directly \u2014 meaning a same-process function call from the hook isn't mechanically possible without adding a new MCP-callable surface. If `sam_schema/server.py`'s `sam_task` write handler itself performs invalidation (the more natural single-point-of-truth design, consistent with \\\"every mutation path... is a source of invalidation\\\" being phrased around handlers, not every individual caller), the hook needs **no direct change at all**. Recommend this be resolved explicitly during S2 planning/architecture rather than carried forward as an assumed task.\\n3. **The read-path parameter/response-shape change (`backlog_view`, `artifact_read`, CLI equivalents \u2014 section 1a) was absent from the first pass of this analysis** and is corrected here. It is at least as large a surface as the storage module itself and must appear in task decomposition as its own line of work, not as an implicit side effect of building the store.\\n4. **Two backlog items (#3055, #3086) share surface-level \\\"content identity\\\" vocabulary with this item but are confirmed structurally unrelated** (sha256 artifact `content_revision` vs. SQLite `content_id` cache key) \u2014 worth an explicit non-relatedness note in the plan so a future reader doesn't conflate them.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T09:18:27.441656Z</sub>\\n\\n## Codebase-side implementation research (wave 0)\\n\\nScope: where Navigation-stage code lives, which write paths need an invalidation hook, what\\n`state_root()` returns and how existing SQLite state follows (or doesn't follow) it, and what test\\npatterns exist for backend/provider/SQLite code in this plugin. Architecture (schema, eviction,\\n`content_id`) is already decided in ADR-3082-1 \u2014 this section only covers implementation-site facts.\\n\\n### Navigation stage location\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/` \u2014 package with `navigator.py` (`ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`\\nfacade, 505 lines), `providers.py` (`MarkdownContentProvider` protocol + `CallableMarkdownContentProvider` +\\n`MCPMarkdownContentProvider`), plus `indexer.py`, `parser.py`, `pagination.py`, `list_navigator.py`,\\n`tokenizer.py`, `renderers.py`, `codeblocks.py`, `links.py`, `models.py`, `exceptions.py`.\\n\\n`ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.load(source, **provider_kwargs)` (navigator.py) is the single chokepoint that\\ncalls the injected `provider.get_markdown()` (Collection), then `MarkdownItParser.parse()` + `MarkdownIndexer.build()`\\n(Generation), and stores the result on `self._document`. Every navigation method (`map`, `links`, `view_section`,\\n`view_code`, `search_sections`) reads `self._document` \u2014 none of them re-invoke `load()`. This means the control\\nset sits **between `load()`'s two halves**: a `content_id`-keyed read/write wraps the provider call + parse\\nstep, not the navigation methods themselves. `providers.py` has no state and nothing to change structurally \u2014\\n`MarkdownContentProvider` is a plain `Protocol`, so a control-set-aware provider or a wrapper around\\n`ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.load()` are both viable insertion points; no existing hook for this exists yet.\\n\\nThere is no existing content-identity, caching, or SQLite code anywhere in `progressive_markdown/` \u2014 this is\\nwholly new code, not an extension of an existing mechanism in this package.\\n\\n### `dh_paths.py` \u2014 `state_root()` and the slug inconsistency\\n\\n`state_root(project_root=None)` returns `_dh_user_root() / \\\"projects\\\" / slug` where `slug = compute_slug(root)`\\nand `compute_slug()` is `str(project_root).replace(\\\"/\\\", \\\"-\\\")` \u2014 **no `.lstrip(\\\"/\\\")`**, so the slug keeps its\\nleading `-` (e.g. `-home-user-repos-claude_skills`). `_dh_user_root()` is `~/.dh` or `$DH_STATE_HOME`. Every\\nTier 2/3 directory helper (`backlog_dir`, `plan_dir`, `milestones_dir`, `research_dir`, `context_dir`,\\n`reports_dir`) is `state_root(project_root) / \\\"<name>\\\"` \u2014 no directory helper exists yet for a bare file\\n(no `*_db_path()` or equivalent). ADR-3082-1 requires `control-set.db` directly at `state_root()/control-set.db`,\\nso a new helper (e.g. `control_set_db_path()`) following the existing `*_dir()` pattern is needed, plus a\\n`Path.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` on `state_root()` itself (already covered by `ensure_dirs()`, but that\\nfunction doesn't currently create a bare-file path, only directories).\\n\\n**Discovered inconsistency, relevant to placement:** the only existing SQLite state file in this plugin,\\n`dispatch-state.db`, does **not** use `dh_paths.state_root()`/`compute_slug()`. Both call sites\\n(`backlog_core/server.py::_project_stub()` and `dh_core/operations.py::_dispatch_db_path()`) independently\\ncompute `str(project_root).lstrip(\\\"/\\\").replace(\\\"/\\\", \\\"-\\\")` \u2014 **with** `.lstrip(\\\"/\\\")`, so the slug has **no**\\nleading dash, e.g. `home-user-repos-claude_skills`. This means `dispatch-state.db` currently lives in a\\nsibling-but-different directory (`~/.dh/projects/home-user-repos-claude_skills/dispatch-state.db`) than every\\nother Tier 2/3 state artifact (`~/.dh/projects/-home-user-repos-claude_skills/backlog/`, etc.) \u2014 a pre-existing\\ndrift, not something to replicate. ADR-3082-1 is explicit that `control-set.db` must nest under\\n`dh_paths.state_root()` (the `-`-prefixed slug), so implementation should call `dh_paths.state_root()` /\\n`dh_paths.compute_slug()` directly rather than following the `dispatch-state.db` call sites' local\\nreimplementation.\\n\\n### Existing SQLite pattern to follow: `DispatchStateManager`\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py` (`DispatchStateManager`, 741 lines) is the closest\\nprecedent \u2014 a synchronous, standalone (no MCP/FastMCP import) SQLite state manager taking a `db_path: Path` in\\n`__init__`, calling `sqlite3.connect(str(db_path), check_same_thread=False)`, `row_factory = sqlite3.Row`, then\\n`self.ensure_schema()` which runs an idempotent `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` / `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS`\\n`executescript()`. Async callers (`backlog_core/server.py`, `dh_core/operations.py`) wrap every call in\\n`asyncio.to_thread()`. This is a directly reusable shape for the control-set manager. Two gaps relative to\\nADR-3082-1's requirements: (1) `DispatchStateManager` does **not** set `PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL` anywhere \u2014 only\\n`backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py` (the 6-table work-item backend, explicitly *not* to be reused per the\\nADR's Consequences) sets `PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL` \u2014 so WAL-mode setup for `control-set.db` has no in-repo\\nprecedent to copy verbatim, it must be added fresh (`PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL` + `PRAGMA auto_vacuum=INCREMENTAL`\\nper the ADR); (2) neither existing SQLite manager wraps its write sequences in explicit transactions beyond\\nsqlite3's own autocommit-per-statement default \u2014 consistent with ADR-3082-1 deferring that to #3081.\\n\\n### Write paths needing an invalidation hook\\n\\nTwo async MCP servers hold every write path in this plugin that could invalidate a stored `content_id` entry:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`: `backlog_add`, `backlog_update`, `backlog_groom`,\\n  `backlog_close`, `backlog_resolve`, `backlog_sync`, `backlog_strike_entry`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`,\\n  `artifact_register` \u2014 every one of these mutates a backlog item, artifact, or GitHub-synced state that\\n  `backlog_view`/`artifact_read` (per ADR-3082-1, R8's stated future consumers) could have served through the\\n  control set.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/server.py`: `sam_plan` (single dispatcher, `config.action` one of\\n  `read|create|list|status|ready|update|append_task|finalize`) and `sam_task` (single dispatcher, `config.action`\\n  one of `read|claim|state|update`) \u2014 the non-`read`/`list`/`status`/`ready` actions are the write paths.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py` (996 lines): the\\n  confirmed external consumer named in Functional Requirement 8. This hook is a **separate `uv run --script`\\n  subprocess** per invocation (PEP 723, own interpreter) \u2014 it explicitly does **not** write task YAML directly\\n  (`ADR-001: hooks must not write directly to YAML`, stated in its module docstring); all writes route through\\n  `_call_sam_fastmcp()` (a `subprocess.run([..., \\\"fastmcp\\\", ...])` call) into the SAM MCP server itself. The two\\n  write-triggering call sites inside the hook are `_call_sam_task_state()` (task status write) and\\n  `_call_sam_task_update()` (task field write), both around line 452-500. Because this hook runs as an isolated\\n  subprocess with no shared Python state with the SAM server process, it cannot itself hold or import the\\n  control-set invalidation function's live state \u2014 it can only call an entrypoint that is itself\\n  out-of-process-safe (i.e., a plain function that opens the SQLite DB, marks rows stale, and closes it), which\\n  is exactly what ADR-3082-1's design already assumes (every access opens its own connection to one file). The\\n  natural call site is either directly inside this hook (after a successful `_call_sam_fastmcp` write) or inside\\n  `sam_schema/server.py`'s task-write handling that the hook's subprocess call reaches \u2014 both are valid; the\\n  hook's own subprocess isolation means whichever entrypoint is chosen must be importable/callable without\\n  requiring in-process server state.\\n\\n### Test patterns for backend/provider/SQLite code\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_dispatch_state.py` (761 lines) is the direct precedent for testing a\\n  new control-set SQLite manager: one `tmp_path`-backed `DispatchStateManager` instantiated fresh per test (no\\n  shared fixture, no mutable shared state), grouped into `Test*` classes per concern (e.g. `TestEnsureSchema`),\\n  each test docstring following a `Tests:` / `How:` / `Why:` structure. `os.kill` is patched via `pytest-mock`'s\\n  `MockerFixture` for liveness checks \u2014 analogous pattern available for mocking time-based eviction/TTL checks.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sqlite_backend.py` covers the other existing SQLite backend\\n  (`backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py`, the 6-table work-item schema) \u2014 useful for WAL-mode and\\n  schema-migration test patterns specifically, even though that backend itself must not be reused per the ADR.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/progressive_markdown/` holds the existing\\n  `progressive_markdown` unit tests (`test_ambiguous_section_ref.py`, `test_indexer_decomposition.py`,\\n  `test_init_docstring.py`, `test_navigation_result_unification.py`) \u2014 this is where new control-set-integration\\n  tests for the Navigation stage belong, alongside `tests_sam/test_progressive_markdown.py` (SAM-level\\n  integration tests for the same package, a separate directory).\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T21:16:48.913770Z</sub>\\n\\n**Status: ALIGNED**\\n\\n#3062's expanded scope (build SQLite content-identity control set + migrate backlog_view and artifact_read onto ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator) directly fulfills architectural direction already established in this plugin's design documentation.\\n\\n**Alignment to component-architecture.md:**\\n\\nComponent Architecture Map, line 26\u201345, states explicitly about `backlog_core`'s current pagination logic:\\n> \\\"That logic duplicates this package's `Navigator` and `Paginator` and is **scheduled for deletion, not maintenance**. A second implementation of addressing or pagination is a defect regardless of whether it works.\\\"\\n\\n#3062's expansion to migrate `backlog_view` (backlog_core/server.py) onto ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator IS that scheduled deletion. This item doesn't introduce new duplication \u2014 it consolidates existing duplication as already mandated by the architectural design.\\n\\n**Alignment to Agent Markdown Consumption Contract (R1 and R8):**\\n\\n- **R1 \u2014 One markdown engine, all consumption:** \\\"No component may hand-build a table of contents, a section listing, a content excerpt, or a bounded response of its own. Where such an implementation exists today it is deleted, not maintained in parallel.\\\"\\n\\n  #3062 implements this deletion by removing backlog_core's duplicate pagination and routing through the single engine (ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator).\\n\\n- **R8 \u2014 Paginated content is addressed by content identity:** Requires paginated responses to carry a stable identifier derived from content + command, enabling re-request without re-collection.\\n\\n  The SQLite control set (ADR-3082-1) is the storage mechanism that realizes R8: it stores generated content keyed by content_id, enables cheap re-paging without re-collection or re-parsing, and will be globally visible to backlog_view and artifact_read once wired in.\\n\\n**Cross-reference to prior merged work:**\\n\\nPR #3080 merged ADRs-3075-1 through -3082-1, which finalized the design for content identity, out-of-process storage, and global (not session-scoped) keying. #3062 is the implementation of those ADRs' design decisions, not a new architectural direction.\\n\\n**Conclusion:**\\n\\n#3062 aligns with stated design intent on all dimensions: architectural principle (consolidate duplication), normative contract requirement (R1, R8), and prior decision closure (merged PR #3080). No divergence detected.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] A paginated response returns a stable identifier derived from the content it paginates\\n- [ ] A later page is requested with that identifier plus a page selector or address\\n- [ ] Serving a later page does not re-collect from the provider and does not re-parse\\n- [ ] A request whose identifier no longer matches current content is reported stale, not served\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- Intended behaviour is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`. Component ownership is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md`. Parent: #3054.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Decisions\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCache scope (session-lived or persisted) and whether the identifier derives from raw markdown or the parsed tree are recorded as open questions in the contract.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"91cd0258ac3f3ab5fe2a80b290b3885b8e15213d11b60f967cf182cf6e0dca2b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABP5YXuQ\"}"}